WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.443
When people use different words for the same idea, useful decisions can

00:00:04.443 --> 00:00:06.190
become surprisingly hard to find.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.852
Here is our community garden brief. It records three risks, and the

00:00:10.852 --> 00:00:13.553
decisions we have made to deal with each one.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.996
I add concern as an alternative to risk. Now I can swap

00:00:18.996 --> 00:00:22.742
between the two words, choosing the wording that fits without losing the

00:00:22.742 --> 00:00:23.366
other option.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.840
The editor connects those alternatives to one idea in the knowledge graph.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.047
Here is the actual synonym definition: risk and concern belong together.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.059
I download that graph and the finished text. Notice that I leave

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:43.915
risk in the document. The word concern is absent.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:50.497
Now I search for concern using the graph I just downloaded. It

00:00:50.497 --> 00:00:54.744
finds all three risk passages, including the related decisions, even though the

00:00:54.744 --> 00:00:55.805
wording is different.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.632
Without synthesis, I get the original passages and their locations. Adding the

00:01:02.632 --> 00:01:07.014
answer flag gives me a summary from the same evidence, with source

00:01:07.014 --> 00:01:07.379
references.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.735
The summary keeps decisions distinct from completed actions. From choosing a word

00:01:14.735 --> 00:01:19.219
to finding a decision, the same knowledge graph keeps the meaning connected.
